{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeFRtzqH3PmCyphcxVUPE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01K6CmYbiaDgyVtPd8XZRd1y","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/patrickd/deepseek-v4-flash-0731-cuda; cat > evidence/decode_loop/COMMIT_MSG_1p10.txt <<'EOF'\n1.10: the racing kernel is `hadamard`, which reads the row it overwrites -- and the SERVER has been nondeterministic since 1.12 (15/15 load-pairs byte-identical after, 0/15 before)\n\nWHAT IT IS. `hadamard_kernel` gives every thread of a row the whole row to read and one element\nof it to overwrite, which is correct only while `y != x`. Three call sites pass the same pointer\ntwice: `compressor.cu`'s `rotate` branch in the prefill and in the single-group emit, and\n`compressed_decode.cu`'s candidate emit. Every other call site passes distinct buffers and one of\nthem carries the comment `// out!=in`. The shipped kernel gives one block one row, stages it in\nshared memory and reduces out of shared, so the read set closes before the write set opens.\n\nWHAT NAMED IT, AND IT COST NO GPU TIME. `tools/lhash_pairs.py` reads 1.9's own logs all-pairs and\nprints the compression ratio of the FIRST differing layer. Only ratio-4 layers run\n`indexer_forward`, and therefore the `rotate` compressor: 335 of 336 divergent pairs across 1.9 and\nthis item's campaign had a ratio-4 first-diff layer and NOT ONE had ratio 128. The six-arm ablation\ncampaign the entry ordered (`scripts/lhash_ablate.sh`) came back ALL-NEGATIVE -- index_score in all\nthree implementations, the radix top-k, 1.12's gemm_fp32 tile and 1.7's sparse_attn staging are\nexonerated by measurement, and the cause was never on a list built from \"which kernel is\ncomplicated\" instead of \"which kernel writes the buffer it reads\".\n\nIT IS A RATE, NOT 1.9's SHARP THRESHOLD, AND THE PRE-REGISTERED PREDICTION IS FALSIFIED IN ITS OWN\nGATE. 200 repeats per row count: 0/200 while the grid fits the 20 SMs once, then 17, 28, 41, 74,\n116, 184, 200/200 with 200 distinct results. A step and a rising rate are indistinguishable at one\nsample per length, which is what a length ladder is. 21 ratio-4 layers turn a 20.5 % per-launch rate\nat prefill 192 into 99.2 % per prefill, which is why 1.9 saw 56/56.\n\nAND \"SO DECODE IS SAFE\" WAS FALSE. The rows=1 shape decode issues reads as 0/200 -- alone. With a\nfiller kernel resident it is 65/200 differing, 28 distinct results, and the engine forks those emits\nonto `g_side` on purpose. Twelve saved server loads, same corpus, same probe: STAGED (1.10's two +\n1.11's four) 15 of 15 load-pairs byte-identical; FLAT (1.10's two controls + 1.12's four) 0 of 15.\nThe split is by DATE, not by arm: 1.12 changed `gemm_fp32`, i.e. what shares the SM. Every server\nA/B through 1.11 reported byte-identical legs and stands; 1.12's is UNPROVEN, not wrong, and is\nre-run as new item 1.15. 1.12's bit-exactness is meanwhile CONFIRMED more strongly than when it\nlanded: the fixed engine reproduces 1.11's four loads leg for leg.\n\nMEASURED, BOTH ARMS ON ONE BINARY (`DSV4_HADAMARD_STAGE=0` is the pre-1.10 kernel):\n  * gate_hadamard_alias: non-aliased output bit-identical to the shipped kernel 200/200 at all 21\n    row counts on BOTH arms (so the staging changed nothing where nothing was wrong); the gate\n    returns non-zero if the OFF arm ever stops reproducing the defect; --control flips one ulp and\n    requires every comparison to see it. 20 in-situ gate runs pass on both arms.\n  * engine, prefill: R protocol 0/56 pairs (was 56/56), W ladder 0/8 lengths (was 2/8).\n  * engine, token ids: 4 identical points at ctx 1,023 x 256 generated tokens in ONE process --\n    6/6 pairs diverge with the flat kernel, first at generated token 26/42/45 (1.0 recorded 43),\n    0/6 with the staged one.\n  * speed: a NULL, drift-free over two arm orders and four loads, -0.085 +/- 1.810 ms/forward,\n    10/18 legs faster; tau 1.6445 staged vs 1.6461 flat. ONE ordering said +1.731 with a drift term\n    of +1.817 -- traps §33 for the third time, here manufacturing a regression rather than hiding a\n    win. Tracked `a` and `b` are unchanged.\n\nInvariant 1's 160-position bound is lifted. wiki/kernel-optimisations.md §2.12,\nwiki/measurement-and-traps.md §36 and a correction to §35 (which blamed the identity failure on a\ncode path the server does not execute), wiki/negative-results.md §4j, wiki/README.md.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nEOF\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport json,datetime\nrec={\"iter\":10,\"rc\":0,\"post_ok\":True,\"commit\":\"pending\",\"dirty\":True,\n     \"log\":\"evidence/decode_loop/iter10.log\",\"ts\":datetime.datetime.now().astimezone().strftime(\"%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S%z\"),\n     \"item\":\"1.10\",\"result\":\"hadamard(y,y) read/write race named and fixed; server determinism restored 0/15 -> 15/15 load-pairs; speed a drift-free null -0.085 +/- 1.810 ms/forward\"}\nopen('evidence/decode_loop.jsonl','a').write(json.dumps(rec)+\"\\n\")\nprint(\"appended\")\nPY\nwc -l evidence/decode_loop.jsonl","description":"Write commit message and loop record"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":318,"cache_read_input_tokens":361378,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":318},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"17d5ee56-c565-4475-a9f8-4b3d4b9993e5","uuid":"4e41efff-9741-4eaf-b15a-ad0474aa6159","timestamp":"2026-08-21T06:36:13.660Z","request_id":"req_011CeFRtyLUxWNUR9EnqDQXg"}
