{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeFS1Mom6Brt96Sx23tYB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Iteration 10 is done. Ladder item **1.10 — name the kernel inside `compressed_attn_forward` that is racing** — is complete, fixed, and gated.\n\n**It is `hadamard`, and it is a buffer alias, not a reduction order.** Every thread of a row reads all `D` elements and overwrites one — correct only while `y != x`, and three call sites pass the same pointer twice. The shipped kernel stages the row in shared memory so the read set closes before the write set opens.\n\n**What named it cost no GPU time.** `tools/lhash_pairs.py` read 1.9's existing logs all-pairs and printed the *compression ratio* of the first differing layer. Only ratio-4 layers run `indexer_forward` and its `rotate` compressor: **335 of 336 divergent pairs had a ratio-4 first-diff layer, not one had ratio 128.** The six-arm ablation campaign the entry ordered came back all-negative — `index_score` (all three implementations), the radix top-k, 1.12's `gemm_fp32` tile and 1.7's `sparse_attn` staging are now exonerated by measurement, and the cause was never on a list built from \"which kernel is complicated.\"\n\n**Two of my own claims were falsified by my own instruments, and both mattered.** The pre-registered *sharp* threshold at 20 blocks is a rising *rate* (0/200 → 200/200 with 200 distinct results); a step and a rate are indistinguishable at one sample per length, which is what 1.9's ladder was. And \"rows=1, so decode is safe\" was an artefact of benchmarking the kernel alone — with any other kernel resident, the same call goes to **65/200 differing, 28 distinct results**, and the engine forks those emits onto a side stream on purpose.\n\n**That last one found something nobody knew.** Across twelve saved server loads: staged **15/15 load-pairs byte-identical**, flat **0/15** — split by date, not by arm. 1.11's four loads agree, 1.12's four do not, and 1.12 changed `gemm_fp32`, i.e. what shares the SM. So `dsv4-server` has been nondeterministic since 1.12; every A/B through 1.11 stands, 1.12's band is *unproven* (not wrong) and is re-run as new item **1.15**. Its bit-exactness is meanwhile confirmed more strongly than when it landed — the fixed engine reproduces 1.11's four loads leg for leg. `measurement-and-traps.md` §35, which blamed this on a code path the server does not execute, is corrected.\n\nMeasured, both arms on one binary via `DSV4_HADAMARD_STAGE`:\n- non-aliased output bit-identical to the shipped kernel 200/200 at 21 row counts on both arms; 20 in-situ gate runs pass on both\n- prefill: R 0/56 pairs (was 56/56), W 0/8 lengths (was 2/8)\n- token ids at ctx 1,023 × 256 generated, one process: **6/6 pairs diverge with the old kernel at generated token 26/42/45; 0/6 with the fix**\n- speed: a **null** — drift-free `-0.085 ± 1.810 ms/forward`, 10/18 legs faster, `tau` 1.6445 vs 1.6461. One ordering alone said `+1.731` with a drift term of `+1.817`; taken at face value it would have manufactured a regression. Tracked `a` and `b` are unchanged.\n\nWiki updated in the same iteration: `kernel-optimisations.md` §2.12, `measurement-and-traps.md` §36 plus the §35 correction, `negative-results.md` §4j, and the `README.md` state table (which now carries a second reproducibility row).\n\n**One thing I could not do:** `.claude/settings.json` denies `git add/commit/push` to me. I wrote the prepared message to `evidence/decode_loop/COMMIT_MSG_1p10.txt`, which is the sanctioned handoff — `scripts/decode_loop.sh` commits and pushes it on the iteration's behalf, and I removed the journal line I had appended by hand so the driver's own record is not duplicated. The commit has not happened yet; it will when the driver reaches that step."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":714,"cache_read_input_tokens":369390,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":714},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"17d5ee56-c565-4475-a9f8-4b3d4b9993e5","uuid":"8fe71a37-0b0e-4ba6-8af6-3c8bb562963b","timestamp":"2026-08-21T06:37:32.303Z","request_id":"req_011CeFS1Kh1qLHTRfKJVkXXU"}
